WEBVTT

00:02.669 --> 00:04.337
<i>The whole of humanity</i>

00:04.421 --> 00:07.173
<i>was suddenly turned to stone</i>
<i>for thousands of years.</i>

00:08.800 --> 00:11.928
<i>After resurging in a primitive world</i>
<i>where civilization had been destroyed,</i>

00:13.012 --> 00:15.515
<i>we steadily rebuilt science from the start</i>

00:16.433 --> 00:18.852
<i>until we managed to build a vessel</i>

00:18.935 --> 00:21.062
<i>capable of crossing the Pacific Ocean.</i>

00:26.317 --> 00:29.904
Greetings to anyone in the future
who is listening to me.

00:30.613 --> 00:32.907
I am your reporter, Minami Hokutozai.

00:32.991 --> 00:35.660
This is a recording for posterity.

00:35.744 --> 00:39.956
We are close to solving the enigma
behind the petrification of humanity.

00:40.040 --> 00:44.002
We're heading towards enemy base,
the moon!

00:45.462 --> 00:49.758
<i>As this big project consists of building</i>
<i>a spaceship out of stone tools,</i>

00:49.841 --> 00:54.471
<i>we'll be gathering materials from</i>
<i>around the world, starting with America.</i>

00:54.554 --> 00:59.267
I'm going to check on the crew again
to see how thrilled they really are.

01:00.602 --> 01:05.231
As people across the world awaken,
those who abuse firearms,

01:05.315 --> 01:09.235
and those who try to gain power
and exploit others will appear.

01:10.111 --> 01:14.282
I won't allow that to happen.
I won't allow science to stain with blood.

01:15.742 --> 01:17.827
That is my duty now.

01:17.911 --> 01:19.079
Tsukasa…

01:19.829 --> 01:23.291
America? I've lived there
for about ten billion years

01:23.374 --> 01:26.461
but I find girls abroad cute too.

01:26.544 --> 01:29.130
Also, the Statue of Liberty
is still there, isn't it?

01:29.214 --> 01:32.759
Since it's made of copper.
I'm not sure though. Sorry, sorry.

01:32.842 --> 01:34.594
You're talking too flippantly!

01:35.220 --> 01:39.516
I was allowed on board as a reward
for working hard in the island.

01:39.599 --> 01:42.435
I'll be a lot of help from now on too!

01:42.519 --> 01:45.146
I've only seen you help a lot, though.

01:47.190 --> 01:49.734
My cute recorder!

01:49.818 --> 01:52.487
Are you recording this now?

01:52.570 --> 01:55.490
The sound is carved
on a lacquered wooden tube.

01:55.573 --> 01:58.201
It's easy to carve
and allows smooth recording.

01:58.284 --> 02:00.703
What do you think about
this awesome idea I had?

02:00.787 --> 02:04.082
But I'm asking about your
enthusiasm for the departure.

02:04.916 --> 02:06.960
The only reason I'm helping you now

02:07.043 --> 02:09.129
is because I'm following orders.

02:09.212 --> 02:12.215
How cold… I'd like to hear

02:12.298 --> 02:14.425
about your own feelings and so.

02:14.509 --> 02:18.346
That's it! You'd be a good match to Kinro
since you're both equally serious.

02:18.429 --> 02:19.764
What do you think?

02:19.848 --> 02:21.432
I don't know anything about that!

02:22.016 --> 02:27.564
Kirisame? She's quite skilled.
I'm sure we won't lose

02:27.647 --> 02:29.357
if we keep training.

02:30.066 --> 02:32.152
Still, Ginro…

02:32.235 --> 02:35.655
I mean, what would have happened
if it wasn't for Matsukaze?

02:36.447 --> 02:38.575
Caring for Lord Ginro?

02:38.658 --> 02:43.371
No, I just took the place of his legs.
I didn't do anything special.

02:43.955 --> 02:46.291
Oh, my legs…

02:46.374 --> 02:49.043
I suddenly felt a sharp pain
right before we set sail.

02:49.127 --> 02:50.962
It was terrible.

02:51.045 --> 02:55.842
Just when I wanted to sail far away,
this had to happen.

02:56.634 --> 03:01.180
But then Matsukaze called me, "My Lord!"

03:01.264 --> 03:03.641
and offered to bring me on board.

03:03.725 --> 03:05.685
How glad I was…

03:07.312 --> 03:10.440
I hate this! I'm so bored I could die!

03:10.523 --> 03:12.400
This is when there's nice weather.

03:12.483 --> 03:14.694
How many more days is this going to take?

03:20.533 --> 03:24.537
I hate this! Now I'm so busy I could die!

03:24.621 --> 03:26.372
This is when there's a storm.

03:26.456 --> 03:29.918
How many more days is this going to take?

03:30.001 --> 03:35.465
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA "JUMP COMICS"

05:07.056 --> 05:10.226
-It'll be 70 days!
-It'll be 40 days.

05:13.771 --> 05:14.731
It's terrible!

05:15.648 --> 05:19.319
This is terrible! Senku and Ryusui…

05:19.986 --> 05:21.946
Oh, no, are they fighting?

05:22.030 --> 05:24.324
What?

05:24.407 --> 05:26.617
Fights are not a good thing!

05:28.703 --> 05:31.664
A fight? No, this is a discussion.

05:32.248 --> 05:35.168
Dis… dis… what?

05:35.251 --> 05:39.130
You're right. It was a fight, indeed.

05:39.213 --> 05:43.051
Fights are not a good thing!

05:43.134 --> 05:46.095
Those are some impressive learning skills.

05:48.139 --> 05:50.308
Is this a briefing
about our route to America?

05:51.809 --> 05:55.480
You are indeed a submariner.
Let's get to it right away.

05:55.563 --> 05:57.690
We set sail at Tokyo.

05:57.774 --> 06:00.401
-And our destination…
-Is America.

06:00.485 --> 06:02.528
Yes, right here.

06:04.572 --> 06:08.034
How do we take
the fastest way to San Francisco?

06:08.117 --> 06:11.370
Naturally, it's a straight line, isn't it?

06:11.454 --> 06:13.331
Even I know that.

06:13.414 --> 06:16.375
Wait, isn't that a little too simplistic?

06:16.459 --> 06:18.252
This must be a trick question.

06:19.670 --> 06:23.382
Good answer! It's called a loxodrome.

06:23.466 --> 06:26.511
We just need to move
in the same direction at all times.

06:26.594 --> 06:30.389
It's an easy-to-navigate route
that's been used throughout history.

06:31.099 --> 06:34.102
We will follow that route for 70 days.

06:34.185 --> 06:35.645
Wow, did I get it right?

06:36.229 --> 06:37.063
&lrm;Wrong!

06:38.064 --> 06:40.608
But that was a fun explanation.

06:40.691 --> 06:42.026
The fastest route is…

06:43.611 --> 06:45.404
-…this!
-Why?

06:45.488 --> 06:47.365
That certainly looks longer, doesn't it?

06:47.448 --> 06:49.617
Seeing is believing.

06:49.700 --> 06:52.787
Let's compare them using that globe.

06:57.125 --> 06:57.959
All right.

07:09.512 --> 07:12.515
The route that looked longer on the map
turns out to be way shorter!

07:12.598 --> 07:13.516
It's true.

07:13.599 --> 07:15.852
That's how spheres work.

07:15.935 --> 07:17.770
That is truly the fastest route.

07:18.438 --> 07:22.525
<i>It's a Great Circle Route.</i>
<i>We'll use this shortcut</i>

07:22.608 --> 07:24.610
<i>and get to America in 40 days.</i>

07:25.403 --> 07:28.489
We'll use the loxodrome
and get there in 70 days.

07:28.573 --> 07:32.618
The Great Circle Route
could be the hardest to steer the ship,

07:32.702 --> 07:34.787
depending on our current location.

07:34.871 --> 07:36.664
It'd be a huge burden for sailors.

07:36.747 --> 07:39.417
We'll take the Great Circle Route
and get there in 40 days.

07:39.500 --> 07:41.252
This is a ship of science.

07:41.335 --> 07:44.130
Even if it's a bit crappy,
it has got a great GPS.

07:44.213 --> 07:46.757
I'm ten billion percent sure
that it will keep on the route.

07:47.717 --> 07:49.594
Stop it, you two.

07:49.677 --> 07:52.722
It freaks me out
when our big two are at loggerheads.

07:54.140 --> 07:55.808
Even if we've been trained,

07:55.892 --> 07:58.936
we're still a bunch of novice sailors,
and this will destroy us for sure.

07:59.020 --> 08:01.647
We can't cross the Pacific Ocean
with armchair reasoning.

08:01.731 --> 08:06.736
It'll be 70 days.
A navigator is supposed to be pragmatic.

08:06.819 --> 08:07.987
Don't you think?

08:08.070 --> 08:12.283
Then, we won't make it on time
for the Yellow Dent.

08:12.867 --> 08:15.786
We'll surely make it
for autumn in 40 days.

08:16.704 --> 08:19.957
<i>That's just enough time</i>
<i>to obtain alcohol for the revival fluid.</i>

08:20.625 --> 08:22.919
<i>If we take the 70-day route,</i>

08:23.002 --> 08:25.213
<i>it will be winter and too late for us.</i>

08:25.296 --> 08:29.133
<i>We would then waste a whole year</i>
<i>of our precious time.</i>

08:29.217 --> 08:31.636
So, it has to be 40 days.

08:31.719 --> 08:34.764
I'm being reasonable
on scientific grounds.

08:34.847 --> 08:36.349
Don't you think?

08:41.187 --> 08:44.524
It somehow sounds like
both of them are right.

08:46.901 --> 08:50.154
<i>Ryusui, who is just being realistic</i>
<i>for everyone's sake,</i>

08:50.238 --> 08:53.074
<i>and Senku, who is being logical</i>
<i>for the sake of the future.</i>

08:53.658 --> 08:56.577
<i>There's not an actual correct answer here.</i>

08:58.412 --> 09:00.581
If both are right,

09:00.665 --> 09:03.292
I'm just siding with Ryusui this time.

09:03.376 --> 09:05.962
I'm just not good at working hard.

09:07.004 --> 09:09.799
I'm on Senku's side.

09:09.882 --> 09:12.552
Even if it's just for a day,
I'd prefer to arrive the soonest.

09:12.635 --> 09:16.681
You just like
to get things done right away.

09:18.766 --> 09:21.018
Then we'll double the crew's food rations.

09:22.144 --> 09:23.062
I'm fine with that.

09:23.896 --> 09:27.858
The number of navigable days
will be halved, is that fine?

09:27.942 --> 09:29.986
Yes, as long as we get there quick.

09:31.362 --> 09:34.907
So it will take us ten days less.
That's the limit.

09:34.991 --> 09:38.995
I will not enterain any more cuts
for the sake of my crew.

09:39.620 --> 09:42.081
Even if I have to fight you, Senku.

09:42.164 --> 09:45.126
My time limit is 40 days.

09:49.255 --> 09:51.382
-Shall we fight then?
-Shall we fight then?

09:51.465 --> 09:54.218
This is getting out of control!

09:54.302 --> 09:57.263
-Are Senku and Ryusui…
-Going to duel?

10:10.693 --> 10:13.613
-A duel…
-Of poker?

10:15.906 --> 10:18.200
Why did you put so much effort
into your clothes? Idiot.

10:19.493 --> 10:21.370
It's obvious, right?

10:21.454 --> 10:24.999
To show the gallery
that this is a formal match.

10:25.583 --> 10:28.669
That way, everyone will
follow the winner's point of view.

10:28.753 --> 10:32.006
And it's obvious I am going to win.
If you are wodering why…

10:32.089 --> 10:36.135
Yes! Sorry, Senku.

10:36.218 --> 10:38.721
But this time, I'm on Ryusui's team.

10:40.890 --> 10:43.517
I'm ten billion percent sure

10:43.601 --> 10:46.270
that you're going to cheat like crazy,
you scamming mentalist.

10:46.354 --> 10:48.522
I've got a bad reputation.

10:49.440 --> 10:52.068
Anyone can join me, right?

10:52.652 --> 10:55.905
Oh, dear me.
Looks like I'm taking on a tag team.

10:57.490 --> 11:01.160
I guess you wouldn't mind
if I team up with Senku, then.

11:01.744 --> 11:04.997
Kohaku, it's all down
to your amazing eyesight.

11:05.081 --> 11:07.958
Keep an eye on Gen's habits.

11:13.255 --> 11:15.132
Our route to America is at stake.

11:15.716 --> 11:17.385
It's a showdown between

11:17.468 --> 11:20.137
the leader of the sea, Ryusui,
and the leader of science, Senku.

11:20.805 --> 11:23.015
Is this for real?

11:23.099 --> 11:25.935
I can't tell who is going to win.

11:26.018 --> 11:29.146
It's a bet then!

11:30.022 --> 11:31.190
I'm betting on Ryusui.

11:31.273 --> 11:34.193
&lrm;Gen is with him,
&lrm;he'll be invincible with his tricks.

11:34.735 --> 11:38.697
I'm not really sure,
but Ryusui looks really good at gambling.

11:39.573 --> 11:42.493
There's no way
Senku would lose in a brain game.

11:42.576 --> 11:45.913
Guys, you know nothing
about Kohaku's eyesight.

11:45.996 --> 11:49.583
Are you having fun?
Your own lives are at stake here.

11:51.585 --> 11:53.129
Let's begin, then.

11:54.130 --> 11:56.841
I'll deal the first five cards.

11:58.217 --> 12:01.637
After that, you will play on
by taking turns.

12:01.720 --> 12:04.390
We can decide who'll be first
with a rock-paper-scissors round.

12:04.473 --> 12:07.977
-Good. Let's begin. Rock, paper…
-Good. Let's begin. Rock, paper…

12:09.520 --> 12:12.398
Sorry, I interrupted
the rock-paper-scissors game.

12:12.481 --> 12:13.732
&lrm;How terrible of me…

12:17.111 --> 12:19.029
How interesting.

12:19.113 --> 12:23.868
I see with my very own eyes
that you've just taken a card.

12:24.452 --> 12:27.371
If the top card on the current deck
isn't the ace of hearts,

12:27.455 --> 12:28.998
I'll admit that I've been rash.

12:38.591 --> 12:39.758
What?

12:40.342 --> 12:43.679
What a surprise.
It truly is the ace of hearts.

12:43.762 --> 12:45.848
What an amazing coincidence.

12:45.931 --> 12:48.809
I won't let this humbug of a man
touch a single card.

12:48.893 --> 12:50.561
&lrm;Boy, you're tough!

12:51.312 --> 12:52.146
Raise.

12:52.897 --> 12:53.731
&lrm;Call.

12:54.732 --> 12:55.941
Full house.

12:56.942 --> 12:58.027
&lrm;Three of a kind.

13:02.114 --> 13:04.283
-Straight.
-Flush.

13:05.034 --> 13:07.411
It's been a back-and-forth battle so far.

13:07.495 --> 13:11.248
But Ryusui's got a few more chips left.

13:13.375 --> 13:14.210
Raise!

13:14.835 --> 13:16.962
The stakes are going up
quickly at this point.

13:17.046 --> 13:20.466
Will Senku take on the challenge?

13:21.800 --> 13:24.053
<i>Ryusui's got so much nerve,</i>

13:24.136 --> 13:27.723
<i>I cannot tell even for a millimeter</i>
<i>if he's bluffing or not.</i>

13:31.560 --> 13:32.394
Call.

13:41.695 --> 13:44.823
Did you sprinkle something
onto the card deck?

13:45.449 --> 13:47.368
Sugar?

13:47.451 --> 13:50.287
<i>Are they trying to make the cards</i>
<i>slide awkwardly?</i>

13:50.871 --> 13:53.707
But I did not touch the cards at all.

13:53.791 --> 13:56.168
You can't wave your hands either.

13:56.252 --> 13:57.628
Okay.

14:02.258 --> 14:04.301
<i>Right before…</i>

14:04.385 --> 14:06.804
Kohaku, check Gen's sleeve.

14:09.431 --> 14:11.016
A bettle?

14:11.100 --> 14:13.102
How could anyone cheat with that?

14:13.185 --> 14:17.064
Its antennae react to the cards
marked with sugar.

14:17.147 --> 14:19.650
How many more tricks
are you going to pull?

14:19.733 --> 14:22.945
I'm out of them. I won't do anything else.

14:23.028 --> 14:26.907
Feel free to check the whole deck
if you want to.

14:27.867 --> 14:29.535
Every single card.

14:30.578 --> 14:32.538
&lrm;If you don't mind.

14:41.046 --> 14:44.675
<i>Right now, Kohaku's eyes, my own eyes,</i>
<i>and everyone else's are on the deck…</i>

14:47.094 --> 14:48.762
I put in all my chips!

14:48.846 --> 14:52.016
Ryusui raised his stake even further!

14:52.099 --> 14:55.227
Is he planning to settle it here?

14:55.978 --> 14:58.939
<i>Right now, Ryusui's cards are facing…</i>

15:00.649 --> 15:02.109
<i>in a different direction.</i>

15:03.819 --> 15:06.113
Gen, you switched the cards, didn't you?

15:06.196 --> 15:08.365
What? I've got a bad reputation.

15:08.449 --> 15:11.160
You drew everyone's attention
with that flashy bug of yours

15:11.243 --> 15:15.247
and made us check the cards.
That's the basis of magic.

15:18.292 --> 15:23.130
That's why I thought you were messy, Gen.

15:23.213 --> 15:27.635
You gave yourself away to your opponent,
and then drew the attention to the deck.

15:28.385 --> 15:31.764
Then you switched
four of the cards in his hand at once.

15:31.847 --> 15:35.935
So you prepared all this
to settle the matter in one round?

15:38.938 --> 15:41.148
<i>So what they were planning all this time</i>

15:41.231 --> 15:42.942
<i>are the four aces in his hand now.</i>

15:43.567 --> 15:45.778
<i>He'll settle this</i>
<i>with the highest four of a kind.</i>

15:47.363 --> 15:51.450
<i>So the only chance Senku's got</i>
<i>is a straight flush combo, isn't it?</i>

15:52.201 --> 15:53.744
<i>That's impossible.</i>

16:04.338 --> 16:07.424
The second card from the top…

16:07.508 --> 16:10.552
The fifth card…
The second card from the bottom…

16:11.178 --> 16:13.889
<i>Senku, you have no choice</i>
<i>but to stop here…</i>

16:13.973 --> 16:15.349
Me too, then.

16:19.186 --> 16:20.604
All in.

16:31.615 --> 16:33.409
He went all in!

16:33.492 --> 16:36.412
Senku just bet all his chips!

16:36.495 --> 16:38.414
Are you nuts? Why are you doing this?

16:40.040 --> 16:43.836
<i>Drawing everyone's attention…</i>
<i>The basis of magic…</i>

16:44.420 --> 16:47.297
Well then, we want to change three cards.

16:47.381 --> 16:51.218
So quick! You just took them
from the top, didn't you, Kohaku?

16:52.177 --> 16:54.555
Of course, I've got a bad reputation.

16:56.265 --> 16:58.684
She drew them from the middle of the deck.

16:59.643 --> 17:01.603
Even if that's so,

17:01.687 --> 17:04.440
there's no point in doing it,
since they can't see the cards.

17:05.232 --> 17:09.236
I can't see any marks on them…

17:10.779 --> 17:14.324
But again, it could all just be a bluff.

17:17.995 --> 17:22.750
Interesting! We'll settle it now.
We're both all in!

17:22.833 --> 17:24.710
The die is cast!

17:26.211 --> 17:27.337
Four of a kind!

17:34.595 --> 17:37.222
Royal straight flush.

17:40.809 --> 17:43.228
I'll take all the chips.
The match is over.

17:46.648 --> 17:50.819
<i>Even if there are five aces,</i>
<i>that's good too, right?</i>

17:52.071 --> 17:53.614
How did he do it?

17:53.697 --> 17:56.075
&lrm;I couldn't tell
&lrm;no matter how much I look at them.

17:56.158 --> 17:57.868
Where are they marked?

17:57.951 --> 17:59.953
You won't see it.

18:00.037 --> 18:02.956
The marks of science
are colorless and transparent.

18:03.791 --> 18:08.087
It's Urushiol.
I happened to have some lacquer,

18:08.170 --> 18:09.379
so I extracted them.

18:10.422 --> 18:13.258
Then I set out to find
the key card right away

18:13.342 --> 18:15.094
and smeared it on the edges.

18:16.303 --> 18:18.430
He happened to have lacquer…

18:20.015 --> 18:23.060
I painted it with lacquer
to make it easier to carve.

18:23.644 --> 18:28.273
But that lacquer thing is transparent,
so no one can really see it, right?

18:28.357 --> 18:30.901
Why was Senku
the only one who could see the mark?

18:33.737 --> 18:35.322
<i>I see…</i>

18:36.031 --> 18:40.744
<i>Senku's skin is allergic to lacquer.</i>

18:41.328 --> 18:42.788
The winner is Senku.

18:44.248 --> 18:46.416
You're so swollen.

18:47.835 --> 18:51.713
<i>We'll take the Great Circle Route</i>
<i>to cross the Pacific Ocean.</i>

18:51.797 --> 18:53.924
<i>I'm ten billion percent sure</i>
<i>it'll be tough.</i>

18:54.508 --> 18:56.552
<i>With Ruri's help from the mainland,</i>

18:56.635 --> 19:00.722
<i>we'll take the scientific shortcut</i>
<i>using the GPS to cross the Earth.</i>

19:01.473 --> 19:03.809
I won big!

19:03.892 --> 19:08.063
I lost all my money, damn it!

19:08.814 --> 19:13.110
Next time I'll get it all back!
I just need one more game.

19:13.193 --> 19:14.820
Haven't you had enough?

19:14.903 --> 19:16.613
-I'm fine with that.
-Really?

19:17.781 --> 19:18.824
However…

19:19.741 --> 19:21.285
&lrm;we'll be playing it here.

19:36.800 --> 19:40.095
Have you just opened the casino

19:40.179 --> 19:42.598
you wanted so badly out of a sudden?

19:44.183 --> 19:46.226
Entertainment is a form of logistics.

19:47.144 --> 19:50.189
If our route is going to be
30 days shorter,

19:50.272 --> 19:52.900
this will be a trade-off
for improving the crew's environment.

19:54.109 --> 19:56.862
<i>Increased food rations</i>
<i>in exchange for ten days.</i>

19:56.945 --> 19:59.823
<i>This recreational venue</i>
<i>in exchange for another ten.</i>

19:59.907 --> 20:04.036
<i>And one more… What would you like</i>
<i>in exchange for another ten days?</i>

20:06.163 --> 20:09.917
Food, entertainment,
and last but not least, drinks.

20:14.254 --> 20:17.049
What is this? Is this a new shop?

20:18.175 --> 20:20.469
Bar Francois.

20:21.053 --> 20:22.846
A bar? That means…

20:24.306 --> 20:25.515
Drinks!

20:45.577 --> 20:48.747
-Let's get back to life, everyone!
-Cheers!

20:55.212 --> 20:56.546
Another round!

20:58.840 --> 21:01.677
So there goes the last ten days.

21:02.302 --> 21:06.515
Everything went as Senku wished.
It all went really well, don't you think?

21:09.017 --> 21:12.020
Thanks to the poker showdown,
the casino has become a big success.

21:12.104 --> 21:14.815
They even threw in a bar among the perks.

21:14.898 --> 21:17.859
This way, we can take on
even the toughest route.

21:17.943 --> 21:20.362
No matter who won the game,

21:20.445 --> 21:23.240
I think it would all
have ended the same way.

21:24.157 --> 21:27.911
<i>I mean,</i>
<i>that's when you also count the time</i>

21:27.995 --> 21:30.038
<i>when Ryusui and Senku first bickered.</i>

21:30.122 --> 21:32.791
<i>I guess it took a certain mentalist</i>
<i>to play a trick</i>

21:32.874 --> 21:35.961
<i>to convince everyone</i>
<i>to take the toughest route.</i>

21:36.044 --> 21:37.671
<i>Isn't that an interesting hypothesis?</i>

21:39.840 --> 21:44.136
What are you talking about, Ukyo?
You're overthinking.

21:45.095 --> 21:48.807
I'll just say this one thing.

21:48.890 --> 21:53.478
The poker game in itself
was very serious, for real.

21:54.646 --> 21:58.567
I'm a bit frustrated about that.

21:58.650 --> 21:59.860
A bit…

22:00.652 --> 22:02.237
Senku…

22:04.031 --> 22:07.617
We'll get to the New World pretty quick.

22:12.080 --> 22:14.458
This is so tempting.

23:53.265 --> 23:55.892
NEXT EPISODE

